WEBVTT

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.720
CON eleven eighty seven asked for clearer and more useful Host docs.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:12.660
The related tickets covered setup, account access, daily operations, and error guidance.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:17.220
We updated those pages, then checked what the instructions actually did.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.170
We used real hardware and real Host and client accounts.

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:26.959
I will show how we built that evidence, and how you can review it beside the docs.

00:00:27.609 --> 00:00:30.729
Start by running the unmerged pull request on your computer.

00:00:30.909 --> 00:00:35.829
You need Git and Node version twenty four, with its package manager.

00:00:36.009 --> 00:00:39.059
Copy these commands from the setup page below the video.

00:00:39.239 --> 00:00:46.829
They download the docs, check out the latest published version of pull request one eighty five, and install its dependencies.

00:00:47.479 --> 00:00:49.659
Keep two terminals open in that folder.

00:00:49.839 --> 00:00:52.119
Start the docs in terminal one.

00:00:52.299 --> 00:00:54.939
Then start the review server in terminal two.

00:00:55.119 --> 00:00:57.629
Open the Host overview on port four thousand.

00:00:57.809 --> 00:01:00.889
Port three thousand is the plain docs preview.

00:01:01.069 --> 00:01:05.069
To open this presentation, click Review presentation in the review panel.

00:01:05.249 --> 00:01:10.489
The video, setup commands, and Owner questions are included in your pull request checkout.

00:01:11.139 --> 00:01:13.507
Some claims needed an account check.

00:01:13.687 --> 00:01:19.787
We opened the real Console and checked individual, owner-team, and manager-team contexts.

00:01:19.967 --> 00:01:25.737
For notifications, we found that the documented address and section name needed a correction.

00:01:25.917 --> 00:01:28.747
We updated the docs to match the Settings page.

00:01:28.927 --> 00:01:33.387
We saved a record of what we saw, without personal account details.

00:01:33.567 --> 00:01:35.917
We did not change any notification settings.

00:01:36.567 --> 00:01:41.317
For a command example, we ran the documented offer search through the Vast command line.

00:01:41.497 --> 00:01:43.197
We used a real client account.

00:01:43.377 --> 00:01:46.607
The command completed successfully and returned eight offers.

00:01:46.787 --> 00:01:51.717
We saved the exact command, software version, output, and exit code.

00:01:51.897 --> 00:01:53.527
This was a real command run.

00:01:53.707 --> 00:01:56.767
It checked the search example, without creating another rental.

00:01:57.417 --> 00:02:03.047
The first hardware example used a server with four H100 GPUs.

00:02:03.227 --> 00:02:06.846
We ran an approved modified installer over SSH.

00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:11.476
We then checked the services, the GPUs, and the storage separately.

00:02:11.656 --> 00:02:17.766
Here, Show on page connects the services result to the exact checklist sentence.

00:02:17.946 --> 00:02:21.117
The evidence supports that sentence on this tested host.

00:02:21.767 --> 00:02:23.327
Open the saved service result.

00:02:23.507 --> 00:02:28.967
It shows the command we ran, a successful exit code, and four active services.

00:02:29.147 --> 00:02:35.117
Other saved checks show four GPUs, the Docker filesystem, and project quotas enabled.

00:02:35.297 --> 00:02:40.627
We kept the target, time, source version, and output with each result.

00:02:40.807 --> 00:02:43.951
This gives the reviewer something concrete to inspect.

00:02:44.131 --> 00:02:48.331
A successful service check does not prove that every installation step passed.

00:02:48.981 --> 00:02:53.231
The next example follows a machine from the Host side to the client side.

00:02:53.411 --> 00:02:55.571
We used separate authenticated accounts.

00:02:55.751 --> 00:03:02.031
We listed the machine, read its settings back, found an offer, and created a client instance.

00:03:02.211 --> 00:03:06.671
The saved responses connect the listing and the instance to the same machine.

00:03:06.851 --> 00:03:10.071
This rental check used direct API requests.

00:03:10.251 --> 00:03:13.411
The command-line search we just saw was a separate test.

00:03:14.061 --> 00:03:17.671
We also ran a small calculation on the rented H100.

00:03:17.851 --> 00:03:23.531
The recorded result confirms that CUDA was available and the calculation returned the expected value.

00:03:23.711 --> 00:03:25.585
We then destroyed the instance.

00:03:25.765 --> 00:03:30.325
A separate read and the client instance list confirmed that it was gone.

00:03:30.505 --> 00:03:32.555
We saved those cleanup results too.

00:03:32.735 --> 00:03:35.185
This proves a small real rental workflow.

00:03:35.365 --> 00:03:37.475
It is not a full stress test.

00:03:38.125 --> 00:03:42.525
The third example found a real problem in a documented diagnostic command.

00:03:42.705 --> 00:03:47.175
The host had four RTX six thousand Ada GPUs.

00:03:47.355 --> 00:03:50.875
The host could see them, but the Docker command in the draft failed.

00:03:51.055 --> 00:03:53.665
The error asked for the NVIDIA runtime option.

00:03:53.845 --> 00:03:55.295
We kept that failed result.

00:03:55.475 --> 00:03:58.185
It explains why the documentation needed a change.

00:03:58.835 --> 00:04:04.785
We tested the NVIDIA runtime option, updated the docs, and ran the exact new command again.

00:04:04.965 --> 00:04:09.781
This time the container saw the same four GPUs as the host.

00:04:09.961 --> 00:04:13.731
We also checked that no test container was left running.

00:04:13.911 --> 00:04:18.741
The old failure and the successful retest remain separate records.

00:04:18.921 --> 00:04:24.021
The reviewer can trace the correction back to what actually happened on the machine.

00:04:24.671 --> 00:04:30.147
To review this work, open the docs on port four thousand and click Review.

00:04:30.327 --> 00:04:31.977
Choose the relevant section.

00:04:32.157 --> 00:04:35.501
Show on page highlights the sentence being checked.

00:04:35.681 --> 00:04:39.951
Open its saved result and read the command, output, and limits.

00:04:40.131 --> 00:04:45.001
If something is wrong or unclear, select the wording and add a comment.

00:04:45.181 --> 00:04:47.771
Enter your name and explain the change needed.

00:04:47.951 --> 00:04:51.531
Feedback saves locally and can be exported for Jira.

00:04:51.711 --> 00:04:57.410
To find these hardware test records, click Evidence on GitHub on the presentation page.

00:04:57.590 --> 00:05:03.421
It opens the verification and evidence folders for pull request one eighty five.

00:05:03.601 --> 00:05:08.566
Click Owner questions on the same page for decisions that still need an answer.

00:05:08.746 --> 00:05:13.866
Use that page to see the question, the affected docs, and who needs to answer it.
